\begin{abstract}
Quantum spin ice supports an emergent photon, usually described as a free, linearly dispersing mode of a Gaussian $U(1)$ gauge theory. In that limit the neutron operator creates a single photon and the entire spectral weight of $\Szz(\bq,\omega)$ lies on one doubly degenerate line. We compute instead the full spectrum of the compact gauge theory, using exact diagonalization of the ring-exchange model in the complete spin-ice Hilbert space on 48- and 64-site pyrochlore clusters. The free-photon description survives at small momentum but fails qualitatively at the zone boundary. At $L$, one transverse photon branch is driven to half of its Gaussian energy, becomes the lowest excitation of the model, and carries about half of the neutron intensity. We identify this mode as a roton in the Feynman--Bijl sense: it is created directly by the neutron operator $S^z=E$, appears where the equal-time electric-field correlations are largest, minimizes the single-mode energy $f_1/S$, and is rapidly recovered by a backflow-like variational sequence built from the probe state. Unlike the roton of liquid helium, the correlation peak is not inherited from the underlying classical manifold but generated by the quantum ring dynamics itself. Compactness also splits the two photon polarizations and transfers $13$--$38\%$ of the neutron weight into higher states. An exact charge-conjugation parity separates the neutron-visible odd sector from the even sector accessed by bilinear probes such as Raman scattering and ultrasound. These results show that the emergent photon of spin-$1/2$ quantum spin ice is weakly interacting only in the infrared; at experimentally relevant zone-boundary momenta, its interactions reorganize the spectrum nonperturbatively.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\emph{Introduction.}---Quantum spin ice (QSI) is the canonical three-dimensional $U(1)$ quantum spin liquid. Virtual exchange within the two-in--two-out manifold of the pyrochlore lattice generates a six-spin ring process, whose low-energy description is a compact $U(1)$ lattice gauge theory with gapped electric charges and a gapless emergent photon~\cite{Hermele2004,Gingras2014,SavaryBalents2017,RauGingras2019,Broholm2020}. This structure is supported by unbiased quantum Monte Carlo~\cite{Shannon2012,KatoOnoda2015} and appears equivalently in three-dimensional quantum dimer models~\cite{Sikora2009}.

The photon is visible because the local Ising moment is the emergent electric field, $S^z=E$. Neutron scattering therefore measures electric-field fluctuations through $\Szz(\bq,\omega)$. In the usual Gaussian description, $E(\bq)$ is linear in photon creation and annihilation operators, so a neutron creates exactly one photon and all of the spectral weight lies on a sharp one-photon pole~\cite{Hermele2004,Benton2012,Desrochers2023,DesrochersKim2023PRL}. The resulting linearly dispersing mode, together with its vanishing low-energy intensity, is the central spectroscopic signature sought in candidate QSI materials~\cite{Benton2012,Ross2011,Sibille2018,Gaudet2019,Gao2019,Sibille2020,Smith2022,Poree2025,Gao2025,Gao2026}. Spinon continua form a separate sector with their own spectroscopy~\cite{Morampudi2020,DesrochersKim2023PRL,Gao2026}; here we focus on the gauge field itself.

The Gaussian theory, however, discards the defining nonlinearity of the lattice gauge field. The magnetic flux is angle-valued, so its energy is a cosine rather than a quadratic function. Expanding that cosine gives free photons; retaining it produces photon--photon interactions. There is no general reason for those interactions to remain weak at lattice-scale momenta. The emergent fine-structure constant is estimated to be of order $0.1$~\cite{Pace2021}, semiclassical treatments find $O(1)$ renormalizations of the photon bandwidth~\cite{Kwasigroch2017,Kwasigroch2020,Szabo2019}, and quantum Monte Carlo finds zone-boundary broadening comparable to the photon bandwidth itself~\cite{Huang2018}. The familiar argument forbidding one-photon decay into three photons applies to the linear part of the dispersion~\cite{Kwasigroch2017}; it does not constrain the flat, strongly lattice-dependent part of the band near the zone boundary. The spectral redistribution caused by compactness has therefore remained unresolved.

Here we compute that redistribution directly. We diagonalize the compact ring-exchange theory in the full spin-ice configuration space and compare it with the Gaussian theory quantized on the identical finite lattice. The comparison isolates compactness without changing either the Hilbert-space constraint or the lattice geometry. Our central result is that one transverse photon branch develops a roton at the $L$ point: a finite-momentum collective mode pushed to roughly half of the free-photon energy, carrying about half of the neutron response, and becoming the lowest excitation in the model. The same interaction physics splits the photon polarizations and transfers a substantial fraction of the spectral weight into higher states. The result is a simple physical picture: the free photon is protected in the infrared, but at the zone boundary the compact gauge field behaves as a strongly interacting quantum medium.

\emph{Compact gauge model.}---We study the minimal ring-exchange Hamiltonian restricted to the spin-ice manifold,
\begin{equation}
 H=-K\sum_p\left(W_p+W_p^\dagger\right),
 \qquad
 W_p=S_1^+S_2^-S_3^+S_4^-S_5^+S_6^- ,
 \label{eq:ring}
\end{equation}
where $p$ runs over contractible pyrochlore hexagons and $K>0$ selects the zero-flux phase. When the six spins around a hexagon alternate, $W_p$ moves them coherently to the other flippable configuration; otherwise it annihilates the state. In gauge language
\begin{equation}
W_p=e^{iB_p},\qquad
H=-2K\sum_p\cos B_p,
\end{equation}
with $B_p$ the compact magnetic flux through plaquette $p$. The Gaussian photon theory is obtained by replacing $\cos B_p$ by its quadratic expansion. Every difference discussed below therefore comes from retaining the full compact magnetic dynamics.

The absence of an explicit $E^2$ term in Eq.~(\ref{eq:ring}) is special to spin-$1/2$ QSI. The gauge electric field is the local moment, $E_\ell=S^z_\ell$, so within the ice manifold
\begin{equation}
E_\ell^2=(S^z_\ell)^2=\frac14
\end{equation}
on every link. The conventional term $(U/2)\sum_\ell E_\ell^2$ is therefore a constant and generates no dynamics. Equation~(\ref{eq:ring}) is the complete gauge Hamiltonian at this order. Equivalently, spin-$1/2$ QSI realizes the strong-coupling limit in which the bare electric stiffness vanishes and the photon stiffness is generated dynamically by ring exchange~\cite{Hermele2004,Benton2012,Kwasigroch2017}. This is why a perturbatively weak treatment of compactness is not guaranteed to work.

The model exactly conserves the winding, or electric-flux, sectors of the periodic cluster. We restrict the Hilbert space to the ice manifold, so spinons are absent by construction. The magnetic sector remains fully dynamical, however, and may in principle contain both multi-photon states and magnetic-flux excitations~\cite{Kwasigroch2020}. Noncontractible wrapping loops are excluded from Eq.~(\ref{eq:ring}) so that the finite torus represents the local gauge dynamics rather than additional topology-dependent ring terms~\cite{ZhouKimMask}.

The observable is particularly transparent in this formulation. Since $S^z=E$, the state created by a neutron at momentum $\bq$ is
\begin{equation}
E(\bq)|0\rangle.
\end{equation}
In the Gaussian theory this state is exactly one photon. In the compact theory the operator is unchanged, but the state decomposes into the exact interacting eigenmodes. The entire problem can therefore be read as a single question: what does the electric-field excitation $E(\bq)|0\rangle$ become once the compact gauge dynamics is treated exactly?

\emph{Finite clusters.}---A finite-size subtlety makes the standard small pyrochlore clusters unsuitable for this question. On the 32-site fcc cluster ($2\times2\times2$ primitive cells, 2970 ice states), the connected zero-flux sector is accidentally an exactly 8-regular graph: each of its 384 resonating configurations has eight flippable hexagons~\cite{Hermele2004}. The equal-amplitude state is therefore an exact eigenstate, a Rokhsar--Kivelson term $V\sum_p n_p^{\rm flip}$ is constant within the sector, the spectrum is integer-valued, and the apparent photon does not acquire the generic compact-gauge dispersion. On the 16-site cubic cluster the pathology is even stronger: only six of the 25 transport sectors have any dynamics, each dynamic block contains only eight states with ground energy $-4K$, and the largest sector has no flippable plaquettes at all. These clusters do not faithfully represent the interacting photon problem.

We therefore use a $2\times2\times3$ fcc cluster with 48 spins, $87\,546$ ice configurations, 48 contractible hexagons, and 12 allowed momenta. The ground-state transport sector contains 9104 states and is diagonalized completely. Its ground energy, $E_0=-10.556K$, differs from the equal-amplitude estimate based on the mean flippability, confirming nontrivial correlated ring dynamics. Because the full eigenbasis of the sector is available, $\Szz(\bq,\omega)$ is evaluated level by level without continued-fraction broadening, and the resolved spectral weights exactly saturate the equal-time correlator $\langle0|S^z(-\bq)S^z(\bq)|0\rangle$.

As a size check we also study a $2\times2\times4$ cluster with 64 spins and $2\,756\,394$ ice states. Its ground sector has dimension $249\,180$ and is treated by Lanczos continued fractions after reproducing the 48-site benchmarks to high precision. Momentum assignments are verified with exact translation projectors, and the probe state $S^z(\bq)|0\rangle$ lies entirely in the intended momentum sector to numerical precision.

\emph{Gaussian reference theory and symmetry.}---To define the free-photon reference on exactly the same lattice, we quantize
\begin{equation}
H_G=\frac{U}{2}\sum_\ell E_\ell^2+K\sum_p B_p^2,
\end{equation}
with $B=CA$ and $C$ the alternating lattice curl over the same contractible hexagons. The singular values of $C$ give the transverse photon branches,
\begin{equation}
\omega_\lambda(\bq)=\sqrt{2UK}\,\sigma_\lambda(\bq),
\end{equation}
with $\sigma=3,2\sqrt3,\sqrt{13},\sqrt{15},4$ on the accessible momentum set. Since the electric stiffness in the microscopic spin-$1/2$ theory is generated by ring dynamics rather than supplied as a bare parameter, we fit only the overall scale $\sqrt{2UK}$ to the photon-like exact levels, excluding the anomalous low state at $L$. This gives $\sqrt{2UK}=0.587$. All momentum dependence, polarization structure, and Gaussian intensity ratios are then fixed by lattice geometry. On this cluster the two transverse Gaussian branches are exactly degenerate at every allowed momentum, so any splitting of the corresponding exact levels is unambiguously interaction generated.

A second exact simplification comes from spin reversal. Reversing all spins,
\begin{equation}
S^z\to-S^z,\qquad S^\pm\to S^\mp,
\end{equation}
leaves Eq.~(\ref{eq:ring}) invariant. Every eigenstate is therefore even or odd under this transformation. Because the neutron operator $S^z$ is odd, neutrons couple only to odd states. In gauge language this symmetry is charge conjugation, $C:E\to-E$, $B\to-B$, and an $n$-photon state has parity $(-1)^n$. Thus the one- and three-photon sectors are neutron visible, whereas the leading two-photon sector is exactly absent from $\Szz$ and instead appears in probes that are even under spin reversal, such as Raman scattering and ultrasound~\cite{Fu2017,Simon2022}.

