\emph{Interacting photon spectrum.}---Figure~\ref{fig:main} and Table~\ref{tab:main} compare the compact and Gaussian theories. We resolve the exact spectrum using two complementary diagnostics. First, the neutron operator is projected onto the two Gaussian transverse polarization directions, allowing the dominant exact levels to be associated with the two photon branches. Second, we add a Rokhsar--Kivelson deformation $V\sum_p n_p^{\rm flip}$ and follow each level with $V$. The slope $d(\Delta E)/dV$ measures whether an excitation contains more or fewer flippable plaquettes than the ground state and therefore provides an independent characterization of its ring-resonance content.

Both transverse branches remain identifiable throughout the Brillouin-zone points accessible to the cluster, but they are no longer degenerate. At small momentum the splitting is tiny: on the 64-site cluster it is only $0.2\%$ at $(\tfrac14,\tfrac14,\tfrac34)$. It grows with momentum and becomes order one near the zone boundary. Because the Gaussian degeneracy is exact on the same finite lattice, this splitting directly measures photon--photon interactions. The large 48-site splitting at $X$ is not size converged---it falls from $53\%$ to $6\%$ on the 64-site cluster---and we do not use it for any quantitative conclusion. The $L$-point anomaly, by contrast, is stable with size and has a qualitatively different character.

\emph{Rotonization of the emergent photon.}---At $L$, branch 1 remains recognizable as an ordinary dressed photon: it lies at $1.730K$, carries $32\%$ of the neutron intensity, and is only modestly shifted from the Gaussian line at $2.033K$. Branch 2 behaves very differently. It collapses to $1.024K$, roughly half of the Gaussian energy, carries $51\%$ of the neutron weight, and becomes the lowest excitation of the entire model. A scan over the complete 9104-level spectrum of the ground transport sector finds no state of either spin-reversal parity below it. Its Rokhsar--Kivelson slope is positive, $+0.34$, showing that the excitation is enriched in flippable plaquettes relative to the ground state.

We call this mode a \emph{roton}. The term is useful here not because the microscopic system resembles liquid helium, but because the same Feynman--Bijl logic applies. A roton is a finite-momentum collective mode whose energy is strongly suppressed at a wavevector where the corresponding equal-time correlations are enhanced. In the present gauge theory the relevant density-like operator is not the particle density but the emergent electric field itself. The neutron creates
\begin{equation}
|{\rm SMA}(\bq)\rangle\propto S^z(\bq)|0\rangle
=E(\bq)|0\rangle,
\label{eq:SMAstate}
\end{equation}
so the measured neutron weight of any exact eigenstate is directly its overlap with the Feynman single-mode state. At $L$, the low-energy mode alone exhausts about half of that state. The roton is therefore not an additional particle unrelated to the photon; it is one transverse photon branch reorganized by the compact gauge interaction into a strongly dressed collective excitation.

This interpretation follows directly from the gauge Hamiltonian. The single-mode variational energy is
\begin{equation}
\omega_{\rm SMA}(\bq)=\frac{f_1(\bq)}{S(\bq)},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
S(\bq)=\frac{1}{N}\langle0|S^z(-\bq)S^z(\bq)|0\rangle
\end{equation}
is the equal-time electric-field structure factor and $f_1$ is the first frequency moment. For Eq.~(\ref{eq:ring}), the commutator closes on the plaquette operator,
\begin{equation}
[S^z_\mu(\bq),W_p]=g_p^\mu(\bq)W_p,
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
g_p^\mu(\bq)=\sum_{i\in p\cap\mu}\delta_i e^{-i\bq\cdot\bm r_i},
\qquad \delta_i=\pm1
\end{equation}
alternating around the hexagon. The first moment is therefore
\begin{equation}
 f_1(\bq)=\frac{1}{2N}\sum_p
 \langle W_p+W_p^\dagger\rangle
 \sum_\mu |g_p^\mu(\bq)|^2.
 \label{eq:f1}
\end{equation}
This is the lattice-gauge analogue of the recoil numerator $\hbar^2q^2/2m$ in the Feynman treatment of helium: the numerator is fixed by lattice geometry and ground-state plaquette expectation values, while the denominator is the static correlation strength. Equation~(\ref{eq:f1}) reproduces the exact spectral first moment to one part in $10^4$ at every accessible momentum.

The $L$ point is selected by this construction without reference to the exact excitation energies. Across all 12 momenta of the 48-site cluster, $L$ has the largest $S(\bq)$, $0.370$ compared with $0.19$--$0.29$ elsewhere, and consequently the smallest single-mode estimate, $\omega_{\rm SMA}=1.85K$ compared with $2.4$--$3.2K$. It is also the momentum of the lowest bright exact line, $1.02K$. Thus the peak in the interacting electric-field correlations, the minimum of the Feynman--Bijl energy, and the exact roton all occur at the same wavevector.

The simple single-mode state does not capture the full dressing of the roton. Away from $L$, the ratio of the exact bright-mode energy to $\omega_{\rm SMA}$ remains relatively close to one, whereas at $L$ it falls to $0.55$. This is precisely where the Feynman ansatz is least accurate. In helium the corresponding failure is repaired by backflow. Here an analogous variational improvement is obtained by repeatedly acting with the full Hamiltonian on the probe state,
\begin{equation}
{\rm span}\{H^jS^z_\mu(\bq)|0\rangle\}.
\end{equation}
At $L$ the lowest variational energy converges as
\begin{equation}
1.450\to1.074\to1.034\to1.026K,
\end{equation}
compared with the exact value $1.024K$. The rapid convergence shows that the low state is a collective descendant of the neutron-created electric-field fluctuation rather than an unrelated accidental level.

The analogy with helium ends at the origin of the correlation peak. In a conventional liquid, the roton is associated with correlations already present in the underlying fluid. The classical spin-ice manifold is instead nearly featureless in momentum space: classical loop Monte Carlo on 2048 sites gives $S(\bq)\simeq0.25$ across the zone, while the equal-amplitude ice state on the 48-site cluster varies only from $0.25$ to $0.30$ and favors $L$ by about $10\%$. The quantum ring-exchange ground state is qualitatively different, concentrating $S(L)=0.370$ while suppressing $(\tfrac13,\tfrac13,\tfrac13)$ to $0.189$. The structure-factor peak that drives the roton is therefore generated by the ring dynamics itself. In this sense the roton is a collective property of the interacting emergent gauge field, not a remnant of correlations already encoded by the ice constraint.

The 64-site cluster makes this mechanism predictive within the available momentum set. Its $L$-type momenta split into two inequivalent classes. The class with larger $S(\bq)$ develops a $0.955K$ low mode carrying $50.0\%$ of the neutron intensity, whereas the class with smaller $S(\bq)$ remains photon dominated at $1.998K$. Rotonization therefore follows the interacting structure-factor enhancement rather than the label $L$ by itself. The same connection appears under a Rokhsar--Kivelson deformation: as the flippability-rich side of the phase diagram is approached, $S_L$ grows and the roton softens monotonically from $1.02K$ to $0.73K$ by $V/K=-1.1$, without a level crossing or condensation in the scanned range.

The character of the $L$ mode is also constrained independently. It is odd under spin reversal, as required for a neutron-visible excitation. Its energy lies far below the cheapest three-photon combination of the exact finite-cluster spectrum, while an even-photon interpretation is excluded by parity. Spinons are absent from the Hilbert space by construction. Finally, a state-resolved connected plaquette flux--flux correlator shows a weak, spatially delocalized flux pattern, indistinguishable in character from the photon states and unlike a localized magnetic-flux defect. These observations disfavor both multiphoton and monopole-pair interpretations of the roton itself.

\emph{Spectral weight beyond the photon branches.}---Compactness affects more than the roton. On the 48-site cluster, $16$--$38\%$ of the neutron spectral weight lies above the two dominant branch-resolved groups; the corresponding range on the 64-site cluster is $13$--$34\%$. The Gaussian theory predicts exactly zero such weight for any choice of parameters. Because $S^z$ is odd under charge conjugation, all of this residual neutron intensity lies in the $C$-odd sector. Its natural interpretation is therefore higher odd-particle content built from the interacting photon and roton modes, with magnetic-flux excitations a possible competing channel in principle.

Two observations favor the multiparticle interpretation. First, the state-resolved plaquette flux correlator remains weak and delocalized for every bright residual state, with no localized magnetic disturbance characteristic of a monopole pair. Second, the Rokhsar--Kivelson slopes of the largest residual groups are comparable to sums of the slopes of the dominant branches; for example, the largest group at $(\tfrac13,\tfrac13,\tfrac13)$ has slope $-0.57$. At the smallest momentum available on the 64-site torus, $(\tfrac14,\tfrac14,\tfrac14)$, the residual weight falls to $13\%$ while the dominant line carries $85\%$, consistent with recovery of the free-photon description as $\bq\to0$. On the finite clusters this weight appears as discrete levels; in the thermodynamic limit it is naturally expected to form a continuum.

A perturbative expansion of the cosine does not reproduce the observed redistribution. Keeping the leading quartic correction, $-(K/12)\sum_p B_p^4$, and treating it to first order gives both line splittings and transferred spectral weight below $0.1\%$ at the fitted Gaussian coupling. Even tripling the effective coupling raises these estimates only to roughly $1\%$ and $3\%$, respectively, still one to two orders of magnitude smaller than the exact values. The roton, the branch splitting, and the off-pole spectral weight are therefore not small anharmonic corrections to a free photon. They require the compact gauge dynamics to be treated nonperturbatively.

