\emph{Even sector and complementary probes.}---The states invisible to neutrons can be accessed with an operator even under spin reversal. We use the plaquette field
\begin{equation}
\Phi(\bq)=\sum_p e^{-i\bq\cdot\bm r_p}(W_p+W_p^\dagger),
\end{equation}
which is the lattice analogue of the operators entering Raman and ultrasound responses~\cite{Fu2017,Simon2022}. Figure~\ref{fig:channels} displays the odd neutron channel and the even plaquette channel side by side. Every exact eigenstate belongs to one or the other, never both.

The even-channel onset at $X$ occurs at $2.23K$. Its energy is numerically close to twice the $L$-point roton energy, since $L+L=X$ on the finite momentum grid, but its Rokhsar--Kivelson slope is $+0.30$, close to the roton slope $+0.34$ and far from the $+0.68$ expected for a weakly interacting pair of such excitations. On this cluster the threshold is therefore better viewed as a strongly reorganized even-parity state belonging to the same resonance-rich zone-boundary sector than as a simple two-roton molecule. In the thermodynamic limit, gaplessness implies that multi-quantum continua can extend down to the one-photon line itself. The experimentally useful distinction is then spectral weight and lineshape rather than the presence of a clean energy gap.

\emph{Infrared photon and zone-boundary breakdown.}---The exact spectra separate naturally into two regimes. At small momentum, the Gaussian photon is protected. The interaction vertex is irrelevant in the renormalization-group sense, one-to-three decay is kinematically forbidden on a linear dispersion~\cite{Kwasigroch2017}, and the off-pole spectral fraction must vanish as $\bq\to0$. The 64-site data already show this trend: the dominant line sharpens and recovers most of the neutron weight at the smallest accessible momentum.

At the zone boundary none of these protections applies. The photon band is flat, the lattice-scale interaction energy is comparable to the excitation energy, and the compact cosine cannot be replaced by a small perturbation around its quadratic minimum. The relevant spectrum is therefore not a slightly broadened version of the free photon but a reorganized set of collective modes: split transverse branches, a rotonized branch at $L$, and substantial multiparticle weight. This is also the momentum regime most relevant to finite-cluster simulations and many neutron experiments. The lattice-scale physics is not an artifact of the calculation; it is precisely the regime in which an experimental crystal probes the emergent gauge field away from its asymptotic infrared limit.

\emph{Experimental implications.}---The results have three direct consequences. First, Gaussian fits to neutron spectra are most reliable near the zone center and least reliable at the zone boundary. At the accessible finite momenta studied here, between roughly one sixth and two thirds of the neutron response is redistributed away from a single free-photon pole; at $L$, the ordinary photon-like branch retains only about one third of the total intensity while the roton carries about one half. Any analysis that imposes a single Gaussian photon line in this regime can therefore misidentify both the excitation energy and the spectral weight.

Second, the zone-boundary broadening reported in quantum Monte Carlo~\cite{Huang2018} acquires a microscopic interpretation: compactness splits the transverse photon branches and transfers weight into additional odd-parity states. The broad feature need not signal the disappearance of the emergent photon. Instead it can arise because the photon is interacting strongly enough that its spectral weight is shared among several collective excitations.

Third, charge-conjugation parity divides the spectroscopy between complementary probes. Neutrons and magnetization-based probes couple to the odd sector, which contains the one-photon branch, the roton, and higher odd-particle states. Bilinear probes such as Raman scattering, ultrasound, and magnetostriction access the even sector, including the leading two-photon response~\cite{Fu2017,Simon2022}. A consistent set of features across these channels would provide a direct experimental test of the interacting gauge-field picture. Stray-field noise magnetometry, recently proposed as a photon probe~\cite{Naik2026}, couples to magnetization and therefore belongs to the same odd sector as neutrons.

\emph{Limitations.}---Our conclusions are based on two finite clusters. The $L$-point roton---its energy scale, roughly half-share of the neutron weight, and correlation-driven mechanism---is stable from 48 to 64 sites, as is the existence of substantial off-pole spectral weight. The $X$-point branch splitting is not size converged and is reported only descriptively. The present clusters also sample only a discrete set of momenta, so they do not determine the roton minimum throughout the full Brillouin zone. Equation~(\ref{eq:f1}) itself is valid at arbitrary $\bq$; extending the mechanism into a full-zone prediction requires the interacting ground-state structure factor $S(\bq)$ on larger systems, for example from quantum Monte Carlo.

The ring Hamiltonian is the minimal gauge theory. Longer ring exchanges, generated in a microscopic spin model, are not included. Nor do we address here the quantitative connection to a particular microscopic XXZ parameter regime. In a companion analysis of the XXZ model with torus artifacts removed~\cite{ZhouKimMask}, we find about $10\%$ off-pole weight at $L$ at physical couplings, consistent with the same mechanism, although there the photon-band states contain appreciable virtual-spinon admixture. The overall energy scale $K$ can be constrained independently from thermodynamics~\cite{ZhouAnKim2026}.

\emph{Conclusion.}---The emergent photon of quantum spin ice is free only in the long-wavelength limit. Treating the compact gauge field exactly reveals a qualitatively different spectrum at lattice-scale momentum. The two transverse photon branches split through their mutual interactions, substantial neutron weight moves into higher odd-parity states, and one branch develops a roton at the zone-boundary $L$ point. The roton is a finite-momentum electric-field collective mode created directly by the neutron operator. It occurs where the interacting structure factor is largest, minimizes the Feynman--Bijl energy $f_1/S$, and is recovered by a short backflow-like variational sequence. Its origin is particularly revealing: the required correlation peak is not present in classical spin ice but is generated by the same quantum ring dynamics that produces the photon itself.

The resulting picture is therefore not that a separate roton is added to an otherwise free gauge field. Rather, compactness reorganizes the photon spectrum from within. At long wavelength the familiar photon survives; at the zone boundary one polarization becomes strongly dressed and soft, while additional multiparticle weight appears around it. Quantum spin ice thus provides an equilibrium realization of a strongly interacting compact $U(1)$ gauge field whose nonperturbative real-time spectrum can be addressed directly by experiment and compared with the same physics targeted in programmable quantum simulators~\cite{Bauer2023,Xu2026glueball}.

